# 0072-settings-profile-row.patch -- the game's settings sheet draws a "Profile" row (DM_PRESET: Standard / Memory saver) whose choice gives its values to several rows at once.
# Applies after 0071 (the tree with 0010..0071 applied). UNTESTED ON DEVICE: compiled (dotnet build -t:Compile, ios-arm64, Release); no simulator or iPhone run.
#
# WHY (the owner's request, 2026-11-20): an Ocarina of Time profile modeled on the Apple TV port's memory settings, changeable in the app with a "profile" setting. The rows and their values come from the
#   pipeline (crates/ibuilder-pipeline/src/settings.rs: add_preset_row writes the row into launcher.json with a `presets` map; apply_preset applies the profile at the boot for what the player, env.txt
#   and the environment left unset). Memory saver = DM_LOW_MEMORY 1, Console mode Handheld, Dynamic resolution on, 30 fps, Render scale 0.5 (and DM_PSO_PREBUILD 0, which has no row: the boot sets it).
#   Ocarina of Time (0400486024DEC000) starts at Memory saver (its covered rows' defaults are the memory values, so the sheet shows what the game runs with); every other game at Standard.
#
# WHAT
#   * GameAppUi.cs: LauncherRow gains `Presets` (option -> {env -> value}), read from a choice row's `presets` object by ParseLauncherRow (a malformed entry is left out, a row never names itself);
#     PresetValues(row, option) returns the map of one option (empty for any other row).
#   * GameAppMain.cs (GameAppSettingsViewController): SetValue gives a profile row's values to the rows it covers that the sheet has, saves once, and sets every control again from the values
#     (valueRefreshers: toggle, slider and its label, segmented control, menu button). The rows stay editable one by one; a value equal to a row's default is not written (SerializeSettingValues).
#   A launcher.json without a profile row (a game built by an older app) draws as before; the pipeline writes the new launcher.json at the next boot (its catalogue digest changed).
